actors: Browser | Client app | Lab IdP

step: The user signs in at the IdP
msg: Browser -> Lab IdP: username + password
text: Same as lab 04: the password goes to the IdP and nowhere else. What's new
  is what happens next.

step: The IdP asks what the app may do
msg: Lab IdP -> Browser: consent screen
note: Lab IdP: checks saved consent
text: The app asked for four scopes. The IdP compares them with what this user
  has already agreed to share with this app. Anything new means a consent
  screen. A user who has already agreed to everything skips it.

step: The user decides, scope by scope
msg: Browser -> Lab IdP: ticked boxes only
note: Lab IdP: grants only ticked
text: Each box is a scope. The IdP keeps only the ones that were requested,
  known and ticked. Whatever it keeps goes into the access token, so this
  is the moment a permission is given or withheld.

step: The scopes travel inside the token
msg: Lab IdP -> Client app: access token + scope
text: After the usual code and PKCE exchange, the app receives an access
  token. Its `scope` claim lists exactly what the user agreed to. The app
  never gets to widen that list.

step: The app checks the token on every request
msg: Client app -> Lab IdP: POST /introspect
text: The token is a signed JWT, so the app could just verify it locally. It
  asks the IdP instead, because only the IdP knows whether the token was
  revoked after it was signed.

step: The IdP answers with the live state
msg: Lab IdP -> Client app: active + scopes
text: An active token comes back with its scopes, and the app checks the
  route's required scope against them. This round trip is the price of being
  able to revoke.

step: The user revokes the token
msg: Client app -> Lab IdP: POST /revoke
note: Lab IdP: remembers the jti
text: Revocation writes the token's `jti` (its unique id) to a list. The token
  itself still verifies and hasn't expired. Nothing about it changed.

step: The next request finds the token dead
msg: Client app -> Lab IdP: POST /introspect
text: The app asks again, exactly as it does on every request.

step: The IdP says the token is no longer active
msg: Lab IdP -> Client app: active = false
text: The app ends the session and records a deny in the audit log. Revocation
  took effect within one request, with no waiting for the token to expire.
